{% set local_problem %}
<p>Alabama's humidity is brutal on exposed wood. When gutters clog or leak, water runs behind them and soaks into fascia boards. In Madison's climate, that moisture doesn't dry out quickly — it sits there, creating perfect conditions for rot and mold growth.</p>

<p>We see this constantly: homeowners call for new gutters, but when we get up there, the fascia is soft, water-stained, or visibly deteriorating. Installing new gutters on that? Pointless. The hangers won't hold properly, the gutters will sag within a year or two, and the underlying damage keeps spreading.</p>

<p>Many <a href="/recent-projects/">Madison homes</a> built in the last 15-20 years used lower-quality wood or composite materials for fascia. Combined with builder-grade gutters that leak at seams, these homes are especially prone to fascia damage by the 10-year mark. The good news: catching it early means replacing a few boards, not rebuilding your entire roof edge.</p>

<p><strong>The uncomfortable truth:</strong> About 40% of gutter replacement jobs in Madison also need some fascia work. Not the whole house — usually just sections where gutters leaked or overflowed consistently. We'd rather tell you upfront than install new gutters on compromised wood.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>We start with a thorough inspection. We check every foot of fascia (and soffit if needed) for water damage, soft spots, and rot. We'll show you exactly what we find — you don't have to take our word for it.</p>

<p><strong>For damaged sections:</strong> We remove existing gutters, cut out rotted wood, and install new fascia boards. We use quality primed lumber or composite materials rated for exterior exposure. Everything gets sealed and prepped for new gutter installation.</p>

<p><strong>Then the gutters:</strong> New seamless gutters on fresh fascia, properly attached, properly sloped, designed to prevent the overflow that caused the problem originally. One contractor handling both means everything coordinates correctly.</p>

<p><strong>What we won't do:</strong> We won't slap new gutters on damaged fascia just to close the sale. Some gutter companies ignore fascia problems because they don't do carpentry. We'd rather lose a job than install gutters we know won't last.</p>

<p><strong>The carpenter shortage reality:</strong> Finding a good carpenter who'll come out for 20 feet of fascia is hard. They want bigger jobs. Gutter companies often don't do carpentry at all. That's why we handle both — your roof edge gets fixed properly without coordinating multiple contractors.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been repairing fascia and installing gutters in <a href="/recent-projects/">Madison</a> for over 20 years. We understand these two jobs go hand-in-hand: you can't have reliable gutters without solid fascia, and you can't protect fascia without properly functioning gutters.</p>

<p>Our crews handle both carpentry and gutter installation. Better coordination, cleaner work, single point of accountability. When we're done, your home is fully protected from roof edge to foundation.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> If someone offers to install new gutters and says your fascia "looks fine" without actually pressing on it and checking carefully, get a second opinion. We've replaced plenty of gutters that were installed 2-3 years ago on damaged fascia — the original installer either didn't notice or didn't mention it. Now the homeowner pays twice.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> If you're replacing gutters and your existing ones have leaked for years, expect some fascia damage. It's just the reality. Better to know about it and fix it now than to discover it when your new gutters start sagging.</p>
{% endset %}

